.TH UNEXPAND 1.SH NAMEunexpand \- turn spaces back into tabs.SH SYNOPSIS\fBunexpand\fR[\-t tab1[,tab2...]] [\-a] [file ...].SH DESCRIPTION\fBunexpand\fRis GNU unexpand, from the textutils, the reverse of\fBexpand\fR.\fRIt copies each named file, or the standard input, to the standard outputwith the runs of spaces at the start of each line replaced by tabs, atstops eight columns apart..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-a\fRreplace every run of spaces that reaches a tab stop, not only theleading ones.  Long form\fB+all\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-t\fI n\fRstops every\fIn\fRcolumns, which also implies\fB\-a\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-t\fI n1,n2,...\fRstops at exactly those columns.  Long form\fB+tabs=\fIlist\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfunexpand notes > notes.tab     leading spaces become tabsunexpand -a table              every run that reaches a stop.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOexpand